        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            A lawn sweeper is a simple machine: wheels drive a set of rotating brushes through gears, the brushes flick debris backward into a fabric hopper, and you dump the hopper when it is full. Because the mechanism is so simple, the differences between sweepers come down to a small number of specs that the box rarely explains well. We built this list around those specs rather than around marketing claims.
          </p>

          <p>
            We started with the sweepers that are actually stocked at the major retailers and on Amazon, because a sweeper you cannot get parts or a replacement bag for is a bad buy no matter how well it sweeps. That narrowed the field to three brands that dominate the category — Agri-Fab, Ohio Steel and Brinly — plus a long tail of unbranded imports that we did not include because their specs are inconsistent between listings and their warranty support is hard to verify.
          </p>

          <p>
            For each candidate we compared brush-to-wheel ratio, sweep width, hopper volume, chute width, height adjustment design, wheel type and weight, and we read through owner reviews looking specifically for failure patterns: stripped gears, torn hoppers, height levers that vibrate loose and chutes that clog. A sweeper that works beautifully in its first October and fails in its second is not a good sweeper.
          </p>

          <p>
            We also tested the claims that matter most to real owners. Can it pick up wet leaves, or only dry ones? Will it collect pine needles, which are thin enough to slip between bristles? Does it work for thatch after dethatching? How does it compare to simply using a <a href="/best-leaf-blower/">leaf blower</a> and a tarp? Where a sweeper is the wrong tool, we say so.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, every spec on this page comes from the manufacturer or from consistent retailer listings. Where sources disagree — hopper volume is the usual culprit — we give both numbers or leave the figure out rather than guess.
          </p>
