        <div id="what-matters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Actually Matters: Sweep Width vs Hopper Capacity
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sweep width and hopper capacity are the two numbers printed largest on every box, and they pull in opposite directions. A wider sweeper covers more ground per lap, but it also fills its hopper faster, because it is collecting from a wider strip. If the hopper is not sized to match, you spend the time you saved on laps driving back to the dump pile.
          </p>

          <p>
            The rule of thumb we use is roughly half a cubic foot of hopper per inch of sweep width. A 42-inch sweeper should have at least 20 cubic feet; a 50-inch sweeper should have 25 or more. The Ohio Steel 42SWP22 at 22 cubic feet and the 50SWP26 at 26 both clear that bar comfortably. Push sweepers fall well short — a 26-inch push unit with a 6 to 7 cubic foot bag fills quickly — but that is acceptable when the lawn is small and the dump pile is close.
          </p>

          <p>
            Match sweep width to your mower deck, not to the biggest number available. If you mow with a 42-inch deck on a <a href="/best-riding-lawn-mower/">riding lawn mower</a>, a 42- or 44-inch sweeper lets you follow the same lines you mow, which is the easiest way to guarantee full coverage. A 52-inch sweeper behind a 42-inch deck means you are guessing where the last pass ended.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hopper design matters as much as the capacity number. Flow-through bags, like Agri-Fab's, fill from the back forward, so debris travels to the far end of the bag before piling up. Cheaper hoppers fill at the chute and choke there while the back half is empty, which means the rated capacity is never reached. A wide chute helps for the same reason: Ohio Steel's 9-inch chute is noticeably more tolerant of sticks and cones.
          </p>

